= Solution
{parent=a}
{c}

The <infimum> of $A$ is its greatest <lower bound>: $\alpha=\inf A$ means that $\alpha\leq x$ for every $x\in A$, and that every <lower bound> $l$ of $A$ satisfies $l\leq\alpha$. The <supremum> is its least <upper bound>: $\beta=\sup A$ means that $x\leq\beta$ for every $x\in A$, and every <upper bound> $u$ satisfies $\beta\leq u$. The <completeness of the real numbers> ensures that both exist for a nonempty bounded <set> of <real numbers>.

= i
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}
{c}

Put $s=\sup(-A)$. For every $x\in A$, $-x\leq s$, hence $-s\leq x$, so $-s$ is a <lower bound> of $A$. If $l$ is any <lower bound> of $A$, then $-x\leq-l$ for every $x\in A$, making $-l$ an <upper bound> of $-A$. The definition of <supremum> gives $s\leq-l$, or $l\leq-s$. Thus $-s$ is the greatest <lower bound>, and
$$
\boxed{\inf A=-\sup(-A).}
$$

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}
{c}

Let $M=\max\{\sup A,\sup B\}$. Every element of the <union> $A\cup B$ is at most $M$, so $M$ is an <upper bound>. Conversely, every <upper bound> of $A\cup B$ is an <upper bound> of each of $A$ and $B$, hence is at least both <suprema> and therefore at least $M$. Consequently
$$
\boxed{\sup(A\cup B)=\max\{\sup A,\sup B\}.}
$$

