= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}
{c}

**True.** Write $s=\sup A$ and $u=\sup\{\sup R_m:m\geq1\}$. All these <suprema> exist in $[0,1]$. Since $R_m\subseteq A$, we have $\sup R_m\leq s$ for every $m$, hence $u\leq s$. Conversely, every $x_{m,n}\leq\sup R_m\leq u$, so $u$ is an <upper bound> of $A$ and $s\leq u$. Thus the <iterated extrema of an array> satisfy
$$
\boxed{\sup A=\sup_{m\geq1}\sup_{n\geq1}x_{m,n}.}
$$

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}
{c}

**True.** Set $l=\inf A$ and $v=\inf\{\inf R_m:m\geq1\}$. Because $R_m\subseteq A$, every row <infimum> satisfies $\inf R_m\geq l$, so $v\geq l$. But $x_{m,n}\geq\inf R_m\geq v$ for all $m,n$, making $v$ a <lower bound> of $A$. Therefore $v\leq l$, and
$$
\boxed{\inf A=\inf_{m\geq1}\inf_{n\geq1}x_{m,n}.}
$$

= iii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}
{c}

**True.** For every $m,n$,
$$
\inf C_n\leq x_{m,n}\leq\sup R_m.
$$
Fix $m$. The number $\sup R_m$ is an <upper bound> for every column <infimum>, so $\sup_n\inf C_n\leq\sup R_m$. This holds for every $m$, making $\sup_n\inf C_n$ a <lower bound> of the row <suprema>. Taking their <infimum> gives the <weak minimax inequality>
$$
\boxed{\sup_{n\geq1}\inf_{m\geq1}x_{m,n}\leq\inf_{m\geq1}\sup_{n\geq1}x_{m,n}.}
$$

= iv
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iv}
{c}

**False.** Take $x_{1,n}=0$ for every $n$, and $x_{m,n}=1$ for every $m\geq2$ and every $n$. Each row is constant, so its <infimum> and <supremum> coincide. Nevertheless,
$$
\boxed{\inf_m\sup R_m=0<1=\sup_m\inf R_m.}
$$
The two outer <extrema> can select different rows.

