= a
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}
{c}

A <sequence> $(a_n)$ has <limit of a sequence> $L$ if
$$
\forall\varepsilon>0\ \exists N\in\mathbb N\ \forall n\geq N:\quad |a_n-L|<\varepsilon.
$$
For the reciprocal rule, the required condition is $\boxed{L\ne0}$. Indeed, eventually $|a_n-L|<|L|/2$, so $|a_n|\geq|L|/2>0$. The reciprocals are then defined on this tail, and
$$
\left|\frac1{a_n}-\frac1L\right|=\frac{|a_n-L|}{|a_n||L|}\leq\frac{2|a_n-L|}{|L|^2}\longrightarrow0.
$$
A finite number of zero initial terms does not affect this tail <limit of a sequence>; if a reciprocal <sequence> is required at every index, those terms must be excluded or assigned arbitrary values separately.

= b
{parent=2}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}
{c}

Rewrite the <recurrence relation> as
$$
a_{n+1}=\frac12\left(a_n+\frac c{a_n}\right).
$$
Since $a_0=1>0$ and $c>0$, a positive $a_n$ gives a positive $a_{n+1}$. <Mathematical induction> therefore proves $\boxed{a_n>0\text{ for all }n\geq0}$, and also shows that every division in the <recurrence relation> is defined.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}
{c}

For every $n\geq0$, direct algebra gives
$$
a_{n+1}^2-c=\frac{(a_n^2+c)^2-4ca_n^2}{4a_n^2}=\frac{(a_n^2-c)^2}{4a_n^2}\geq0.
$$
Thus $\boxed{a_n^2\geq c\text{ for every }n\geq1}$. This identity is the key estimate in <Newton iteration for a square root>.

= iii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}
{c}

For $n\geq1$, the preceding parts give a positive denominator and a nonpositive numerator in
$$
a_{n+1}-a_n=\frac{c-a_n^2}{2a_n}.
$$
Hence $\boxed{a_{n+1}\leq a_n\text{ for every }n\geq1}$. The restriction $n\geq1$ matters: if $c>1$, then $a_1=(1+c)/2>a_0$.

= Solution
{parent=b}
{c}

The tail $(a_n)_{n\geq1}$ is a <monotone sequence> bounded below by zero. The <bounded monotone sequence theorem> therefore gives $a_n\to L\geq0$. Taking the <limit of a sequence> in $a_n^2\geq c$ yields $L^2\geq c>0$, so $L>0$. The reciprocal rule from part (a) permits passage to the <limit of a sequence> in the <recurrence relation>:
$$
L=\frac12\left(L+\frac cL\right).
$$
Multiplication by $2L$ gives
$$
\boxed{L>0,\qquad L^2=c.}
$$
This proves existence of the positive <square root> without assuming that existence when constructing the <sequence>.

