= i
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}
{c}

**Only (ii) is necessarily true.** Every real $a_n$ satisfies $a_n^2\geq0>c$, so (ii) holds automatically. For a single counterexample to (i) and (iii), choose $c=-3$ and $a_0=1$. The <recurrence relation> then gives the nonzero periodic <sequence>
$$
a_{2j}=1,\qquad a_{2j+1}=-1\qquad(j\geq0).
$$
Here $a_1<0$, disproving (i), and $a_2>a_1$, disproving (iii). This counterexample satisfies the requirement that no iterate is zero.

= ii
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}
{c}

Write $d=-c>0$. Suppose, for a contradiction, that only finitely many sign changes occur. Since every $a_n\ne0$, the sign is then constant for $n\geq N$. Set $b_n=|a_n|>0$ on that tail. Multiplying the <recurrence relation> by this constant sign gives
$$
b_{n+1}=\frac12\left(b_n-\frac d{b_n}\right)>0,
\qquad 0<b_{n+1}<\frac12b_n.
$$
Consequently $b_{N+j}<2^{-j}b_N$ for $j\geq1$, and $b_n\to0$ by comparison with a <geometric sequence>. But positivity of $b_{n+1}$ requires $b_n^2>d$ at every step, which is incompatible with $b_n\to0$. Thus the <negative-parameter square-root iteration changes sign infinitely often>.

